<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Change</h1>
<p>Usage:
</p>
<pre> change &lt;type&gt; [arguments]
 change &lt;words&gt;
</pre>
<p>&lt;type&gt; being one of:
</p>
<ul><li>brief</li>
<li>compact</li>
<li>expert</li>
<li>inform</li>
<li>shout</li>
<li>tell</li>
<li>communications</li>
<li>wimpy</li>
<li>peaceful</li>
<li>prompt (to set what your prompt shows, use the prompt command: see <a cmd='#'>Help Prompt</a>)</li>
<li>exits</li>
<li>columns &lt;number&gt;</li>
<li>rows &lt;number&gt;</li>
<li>terminal dumb, terminal tty or terminal ansi</li>
<li>telnet</li>
<li>character echo</li>
<li>redraw prompt</li></ul>
<p>change on its own lists them. Most of them switch a setting on or off: type
the same again to switch it back. They can be shortened, for example change
comm for communications. communications and character echo are explained in
<a cmd='#'>Help Echo</a>.
</p><p>Anything else you type after change becomes your status in the who list, for
example change looking for a group (see <a cmd='#'>Help Who</a>). If the first word starts
like one of the types above, that setting is switched instead.
</p><p><br />
See also:
</p>
<pre>&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Brief</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Compact</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Expert</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Inform</a>
